## 1. Learning dynamics from small initialisation

### Saxe, McClelland & Ganguli, ICLR 2014 (1312.6120, 2013-12-20)

- Whitened inputs. Each mode u = ab obeys τ u' = 2u(s − u) (Eq. 10), with the closed-form sigmoid u(t) = s e^{2st/τ} / (e^{2st/τ} − 1 + s/u0) (Eq. 12).
- Learning time is t ≈ (τ/s) ln(s/ε), so 1/s.
- Near the origin, u' ≈ (2s/τ) u: every mode is linearly unstable, at a rate set by its signal.
- Deeper networks: τ u' = (N_l − 1) u^{2−2/(N_l−1)} (s − u) (Eq. 15). For depth > 2 the origin is a degenerate saddle and growth is polynomial.

### Saxe et al., PNAS 2019 (1810.10531, 2018-10-23)

- Modes are learned in order of singular value, giving stage-like learning [content consistent; only date re-checked].

### Arora, Cohen & Hazan, ICML 2018 (1802.06509, 2018-02-19)

- Thm 1, end-to-end dynamics: W' = −η Σ_j (WW^T)^{(j−1)/N} ∇L(W) (W^TW)^{(N−j)/N}.

### Arora, Cohen, Hu & Luo, NeurIPS 2019 (1905.13655, 2019-05-31)

- Thm 3: σ_r' = −N (σ_r^2)^{1−1/N} <∇ℓ(W), u_r v_r^T>.
- The force on a direction is its projected gradient, multiplied by σ_r^{2−2/N}. Depth therefore suppresses small directions.

### Gidel, Bach & Lacoste-Julien, NeurIPS 2019 (1904.13262, 2019-04-30)

- Thm 1, two-layer closed form: w_i(t) = σ_i e^{2σ_i t − 2δ} / (λ_i (e^{2σ_i t − 2δ} − e^{−2δ}) + σ_i).
- Small-initialisation limit: each component switches on at a step, at time T_i = 1/σ_i (1/(ησ_i) in discrete time).

### Li, Luo & Lyu, ICLR 2021 (2012.09839, 2020-12-17)

- For L(U) = ½ f(UU^T), the dynamics near 0 are U' = QU with Q = −∇f(0): continuous power iteration (Eq. 3).
- Thm 5.3/5.11: greedy low-rank learning.
- Template: adding weight decay λ gives U' = (Q − λI)U, so direction i grows iff μ_i > λ. This step is our extension, not their statement.

### Jacot et al., "Saddle-to-saddle dynamics in deep linear networks" (2106.15933, 2021-06-30)

- Thm 2: gradient flow escapes the origin along the top singular direction of ∇C(0).
- Escape time is ~ −log α for L = 2 and ~ α^{−(L−2)} for L > 2.

### Atanasov, Bordelon & Pehlevan, ICLR 2022 (2111.00034, 2021-10-29)

- Silent alignment: the kernel aligns at t1 ~ 1/s, before the function grows at t2 ~ s^{−1} log(s/σ^2).
- So the direction is chosen while the representation is still small.

### Simon et al., "On the stepwise nature of SSL", ICML 2023 (2303.15438, 2023-03-27)

- Linear Barlow Twins: s_j' = 4(1 − γ_j s_j^2) γ_j s_j (Eq. 12).
- Endpoint: s_j(∞) = γ_j^{−1/2} if γ_j > 0, and 0 otherwise. The step time is τ_j = −log(s_j(0)^2 γ_j)/(8γ_j).
- This is the closest SSL analogue of "a direction survives iff its drive is positive".

## 2. Weight decay and rank thresholds

### Nuclear-norm variational identity (standard fact)

- min_{AB=W} ½(||A||_F^2 + ||B||_F^2) = ||W||_*, which soft-thresholds singular values: σ ↦ (σ − λ)_+ [standard; Srebro et al. 2005, Cai–Candès–Shen 2010; not re-read].

### Kobayashi, Akram & von Oswald, NeurIPS 2024 (2410.23819, 2024-10-31)

- L(AB^T) + λ(||A||^2 + ||B||^2) has the same local minima as L(AB^T) + λ||AB^T||_*.
- The two coincide exponentially fast during training.

### Ziyin, Li & Meng, "Exact solutions of a deep linear network", NeurIPS 2022 (2202.04777, 2022-02-10)

- Thm 1: zero is the global minimum iff ||E[xy]||^2 ≤ γ_u γ_w (the "overregularized" regime).
- Prop 2: for depth ≥ 2 hidden layers, the origin is always a local minimum.
- Thm 3: a sufficient condition for a nontrivial minimum.

### Wang & Jacot (2305.16038, 2023-05-25) [venue unverified]

- Weight decay induces a Schatten-2/L cost.
- SGD makes one-way jumps from high rank to low rank.
- Small-singular-value critical points: S_ii = (2λ / |u_i^T ∇C v_i|)^{L/(L−2)}.

### Galanti, Siegel, Gupte & Poggio (2206.05794, 2022-06-12)

- Thm 3.3: with SGD and weight decay, rank ≲ m_l B log(2/ε)/(2μλ).

### Ziyin, Lubana, Ueda & Tanaka, "What shapes the loss landscape of SSL?", ICLR 2023 (2210.00638, 2022-10-02)

- Expands the SSL loss around W = 0: "stability at the origin decides the collapse".
- Thm 1: stationary points satisfy W^TW = ½ Σ^{-1} B Σ^{-1}, where only directions with positive eigenvalues of B survive.
- Collapse condition for subspace i, with weight decay γ: ((1−α)/N) c_i + γ ≥ a_i (weighted InfoNCE).
- Contrastive, not JEPA, but the closest landscape precursor.

### Lucas et al., NeurIPS 2019 (1911.02469, 2019-11-06)

- pPCA: component i survives iff λ_i > σ^2.

### Wang & Ziyin, NeurIPS 2022 (2205.04009, 2022-05-09)

- Mode i is nonzero iff ζ_i > √β η_dec.

### Ichikawa & Hukushima (2310.15440, 2023-10-24) [venue unverified]

- The posterior-collapse threshold in an online-SGD ODE is stable iff β < ρ + η.

## 3. EMA, stop-gradient and target networks as dynamical systems

### Tian, Chen & Ganguli, ICML 2021 (2102.06810, 2021-02-12) — DirectPred

- Linear BYOL/SimSiam with EMA target W_a = τW. Predictor and F = WXW^T are eigen-aligned (Thm 3).
- Mode equations (Eqs. 11–13):
  - p_j' = α_p s_j [τ − (1+σ^2) p_j] − η p_j
  - s_j' = 2 p_j s_j [τ − (1+σ^2) p_j] − 2η s_j
- Invariant: s_j = α_p^{-1} p_j^2 + e^{−2ηt} c_j.
- Reduced dynamics: p_j' = p_j^2 [τ − (1+σ^2) p_j] − η p_j (Eq. 16).
- Fixed points: p*_± = [τ ± √(τ^2 − 4η(1+σ^2))] / (2(1+σ^2)).
- When η > 0, the origin is stable with basin p_j < p*_−. Collapse is unavoidable if η > τ^2/(4(1+σ^2)).
- Small τ (EMA lag) enlarges the basin. EMA acts as an automatic curriculum.
- Already a per-mode drive-vs-decay condition, for BYOL with augmentations: no masking, no JEPA predictor.

### Halvagal, Laborieux & Zenke, NeurIPS 2023 (2212.04858, 2022-12-09)

- With stop-gradient and Euclidean loss: z_m' = η λ_m (1 − λ_m) z_m.
- Without stop-gradient: z_m' = −η (1 − λ_m)^2 z_m, so every mode collapses.

### Tang et al., "Understanding self-predictive learning for RL", ICML 2023 (2212.03319, 2022-12-06)

- Thm 1: with an optimal (fast) predictor and semi-gradient, Φ^TΦ is conserved.
- A perfectly fitted predictor cancels the radial drive. Growth needs a lagging predictor or target.

